1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The following taken from ouse of Chinn at
2 CONT
2 CONT http://houseofchinn.com/index.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Toward the latter part of its history Chen Guo was not always a harmonious state but saw many palace intrigues involving assassinations and persistent attacks by its southern neighbour, Chu Guo (楚國). In 700BC one of those who died
2 CONC  was the 14th marquis, Chen Ligong (陳歷公)/Gui Yue (媯躍) whose reign was cut short to five months.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then in 673BC more assassinations arose during a power grab. Chen Ligong’s son, Chen Wan (陳完), style name Jingzhong (敬仲), fearing for his life, fled and took refuge in the neighbouring northern state of Qi Guo (齊國) in Shandong Pro
2 CONC vince. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Once there he changed his surname from 陳 to 田 (today pronounced Tian) to conceal his identity. In those days, both of these surnames had roughly the same pronunciation Dien. This simple act resulted in the creation of a new surnam
2 CONC e, Tian and hence its blood ties with the Chen clan. The ruling family of Qi Guo was Jiang (姜) and although they recognized Chen Wan was nobility, they only appointed him a minor official in the Qi court.
