1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''The 50th Generation Ancestor: 員餘 (Yuán Yú)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also known as 譚員 (Tán Yuán), as his courtesy name was 子彬 (Zi Bīn).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in 915 BC. He was appreciated by Emperor Xuan of Zhou 周宣王 (Zhōu Xuān Wáng), so the emperor let his sister Princess Ying married Tan Yuan and conferred him a title 子侯 (Zi Hóu), the ruler of Tan County. He continued the 
2 CONC edition and finished it from the 5th generation to the 10th generation. He also established the name order from the 9th to the 16th. That's 日月草木 (Rì, Yuè, Cǎo, Mù), 花開賢祁 (Huā, Kāi, Xián, Qí). 
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife, 姬 (Ms. Jī), Princess Ying, she had three sons named 日龍 (Rì Lóng), 日華 (Rì Huá), 日興 (Rì Xìng), and two daughters. She was buried at the south of 甲水 (Jiǎ Shuǐ) with her husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sixth edition of the ancient lineage Chief Editor: The 50th Generation Ancestor 員餘 (Yuán Yú), he continued and finished the edition from the 48th Generation to the 52nd Generation. He also established the name order from the 9
2 CONC th Generation to the 16th Generation. That's 日月草木 (Rì, Yuè, Cǎo, Mù), 花開賢祁 (Huā, Kāi, Xián, Qí).
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife, 白 (Ms. Bái), had four sons named 平東 (Píng Dōng), 平南 (Píng Nán), 平西 (Píng Xī), and 平北 (Píng Běi).
