1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Xiao Changmao''' 蕭長懋 (458–493), ''zi'' '''Yunqiao''' 雲喬, nickname Baize 白澤. Southern Qi prince and literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Changmao's original ancestral home was Lanling 蘭陵 in Donghai 東海 (modern Shandong area). His ancestors moved to Nan Lanling  南蘭陵 (northwest of modern Changzhou, Jiangsu) during the Eastern Jin period. He was the eldest son of 
2 CONC Xiao Ze 蕭賾 (440–493), Emperor Wu  (r. 482–493) of the Southern Qi dynasty. He is also known as Crown Prince '''Wenhui''' 文惠. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Changmao was born before his father Xiao Ze reached the age of twenty. He reputedly was more handsome than his grandfather, Xiao Daocheng 蕭道成 (427–482), Emperor Gao (r. 479–482), the founder of the Southern Qi dynasty. Xiao D
2 CONC aocheng was very fond of Xiao Changmao. During the late Yuanhui (473–477) period of the Liu-Song, Xiao Changmao was offered the position of assistant in the palace library. He declined the offer and was named Bulwark-general. Late
2 CONC r he was transferred to the staff of Liu Xie 劉燮 (fl. 470), Prince of Jinxi 晉熙. In 477, the Liu-Song general Shen Youzhi 沈攸之 (d. 478) launched an attack against Xiao Daocheng for killing the Liu-Song Emperor Houfei 後廢 (r. 473–477).
2 CONC  Xiao Changmao and his father defeated and killed Shen Youzhi. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Changmao then returned to the capital. At that time, Xiao Daocheng was laying the foundation for a new regime. He said to his confidant Xun Boyu 荀伯玉 (434–483): “When I am travelling, Changmao shall command all troops inside t
2 CONC he capital city. Even when I am not travelling, make sure that Changmao attends to matters of defense externally and internally and armored troops at all gates” (''Nan shi'' 44.1098).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 479, Xiao Daocheng formally established his own regime, the Southern Qi dynasty. He appointed Xiao Changmao regional inspector of Yongzhou 雍州 (modern Hubei area) because the administrative headquarters Xiangyang 襄陽 of Yongzhou 
2 CONC was a place of strategic importance. Emperor Gao did not wish to have anyone else in charge of such an important city. In addition, Emperor Gao assigned Xiao Changmao as commander-in-chief, leader of court gentleman of the north, 
2 CONC and Commandant Who Pacifies the Man People. In the fourth month of 479, Xiao Changmao was awarded the title Prince of Nan commandery 南郡. This was the first time that the oldest grandson of an emperor was conferred the title “princ
2 CONC e” in a southern dynasty. In 480, Xiao Changmao was summoned to the capital where he served as palace attendant as well as general of capital army. When his father Xiao Ze was enthroned as Emperor Wu, Xiao Changmao was named heir 
2 CONC designate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Gao had a strong interest in the ''Zuoshi chunqiu'' 左氏春秋 (Zuo Tradition of the Spring and Autumn Annals). He had Xiao Changmao recite the passages from this book. Xiao Changmao was a man of many talents. He was a skilled m
2 CONC usician, archer, and had a great capacity for alcohol. He had a graceful and elegant bearing and carried himself with ease. He was a glib conversationalist and was on good terms with both literary and military men. One of his earl
2 CONC y scholar friends was Shen Yue 沈約 (441–513). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 485, Xiao Changmao lectured on the ''Xiao jing'' 孝經 (Classic of Filial Piety) at the Chongzheng 崇正 Hall. Wang Qian 王僉 (504–548) ordered Zhou Yong 周顒 (441?–491?), who was a member of the Xiao Changmao's staff, to compile his lec
2 CONC tures into a set of commentaries to the ''Xiao jing''. Xiao Changmao directed Yu Yan 虞炎 (fl. 479–495) to edit the collected works of Bao Zhao 鮑照 (ca. 414–466). During the last years of his rule, Emperor Wu turned over much of the 
2 CONC court administration to Xiao Changmao. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Changmao and his younger brother Xiao Zilang 蕭子良 (460–494), Prince of Jingling 竟陵, were devout Buddhists. They established the Liuji Guan 六疾館 (Six-illnesses Institute) to support the poor and treat them when they were sick. H
2 CONC owever they both enjoyed an extravagant life style. Their father severely criticized them for the lavish buildings that they had constructed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Changmao was a skilled writer. In 492, Emperor Gao's second son Xiao Ni 蕭嶷 (444–492), Prince of Yuzhang 豫章, died. Xiao Changmao wrote a grave inscription to commemorate his uncle's achievements. Before the epitaph could be in
2 CONC scribed on a stone tablet, Xiao Changmao died of illness the following year. Xiao Changmao had a collection of writings in eleven ''juan'' which has been lost. Yan Kejun has collected his only extant prose work, “Bin du shang biao
2 CONC ” 病篤上表 (Submitting a petition while being mortally ill) in his ''Quan shanggu Sandai Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Mather, The Poet Shen Yüeh, 22–25, 84–87, 200–1. 
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhonguo wenxuejia dacidian, 382.
2 CONT
