1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xie Jiqing 謝幾卿 (b. 476). Southern Qi and Liang period literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Jiqing's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 in Chen 陳 commandery. His father was Xie Chaozong 謝超宗 (d. 483), the grandson of Xie Lingyun 謝靈運 (385–433). In 483, Xie Chaozong was sentenced to exile in Yuezhou 越州 (administrative seat n
2 CONC orthwest of modern Hepu 河浦, Guangxi). The imperial edict did not allow family members to accompany him. The eight-year-old Xie Jiqing saw his father off at Xinting 新亭 Island in the south-western suburbs of Jiankang. He was so grie
2 CONC f stricken, he jumped into the Yangzi River. Companions were able to rescue him before he drowned. On his way to his exile, Xie Chaozong was ordered to commit suicide. Upon hearing of his father's death, Xie Jiqing mourned “beyond
2 CONC  the bounds of ritual propriety.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 487, Xie Jiqing entered the national university as a student. He was only twelve sui at the time. In the winter of this year, the crown prince Xiao Changmao 蕭長懋 (458–493) oversaw the examinations. He said to the chancellor of t
2 CONC he university Wang Jian 王儉 (452–489), who had once been the victim of one of Xie Chaozong's witty remarks, “Jiqing truly has mastered the principles of arcane learning. Now you can question him about the meaning of the Classics.” 
2 CONC Wang Jian tested him, and Jiqing was able to reply eloquently. Xiao Changmao exclaimed, “Xie Chaozong has not died after all!” 
2 CONT
2 CONT As he grew older, Xie Jiqing was known for his broad learning and abil-ity to write with elegance and grace. He began his official career as assistant in-ordinary on the staff of the Prince of Yuzhang. He was soon promoted to aide
2 CONC  in the judicial section of the staff of the Chariot and Horse General, followed by an appointment as assistant to the counselor-in-chief. He later was assigned as magistrate of Ningguo 寧國 (modern Ningguo, Anhui). At the end of th
2 CONC e Southern Qi, Xie Jiqing served as recorder on the staff of the Prince of Jin'an, Xiao Baoyi 蕭寶義 (fl. 494–501). 
2 CONT
2 CONT With the founding of the Liang in 502, Xie Jiqing was appointed secre-tarial aide to Xiao Hui 蕭恢, Prince of Poyang 鄱陽, the younger brother of Emperor Wu (r. 502–549). He then was assigned as director of the Section for the Three E
2 CONC xcellencies of the Department of State Affairs, and was transferred to the post of secretarial censor. Although this position was the same six-grade rank as his previous position, it was considered much less prestigious. Appointme
2 CONC nt to this post was jocularly referred to as “southern flight” (''nan ben'' 南奔). Displeased with his appointment, Xie Jiqing frequently excused himself from performing his duties on the grounds of illness. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Jiqing subsequently was named to various other posts including gentleman cavalier attendant, director of the Secretariat, professor of the national university, and assistant director of the left in the Department of State Affa
2 CONC irs. Like his great-grandfather and father, Xie Jiqing did not always follow court protocol, and he often neglected his duties. Once, after participating in a banquet in the Leyou Park, not satisfied with the amount of alcohol ser
2 CONC ved at the affair, on the way home he halted his chariot in front of a wine shop. Seated on the chariot, he drank with his three grooms. They soon were surrounded by a wall of onlookers. Xie continued drinking without seeming to n
2 CONC otice them. On another occasion, the officials in the Department of State Affairs had gathered to deliberate about an important matter. Xie Jiqing arrived still hungover from a drinking bout. In the middle of the group, he put his
2 CONC  head on a pillow and lay down as if no one were around. He finally tried the patience of the authorties when he was on duty at night in the Department of State Affairs. He put on an apron, and climbed up on the raised passageway 
2 CONC where he drank and shouted with some of his students. For this offense he was impeached and removed from office. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly thereafter Xie Jiqing returned to official service again as professor of the national university. He was assigned as governor of Hedong 河東 (administrative seat west of modern Songci 松濨, Hubei). Before completing his term o
2 CONC f service, Xie requested to be relieved of his duties. Ca. 520, Xie Jiqing was appointed director of the Court of the Watches in the household of the crown prince Xiao Tong 蕭統 (501–531). In 524, he served as senior admnistrator on
2 CONC  the staff of Xiao Wei 蕭偉 (476–533), a younger brother of Emperor Wu. In 525, Xiao Yuanzao 蕭淵藻 (483–549) was about to set off to the north on a military expedition. Xie Jiqing requested to accompany him. After the Liang army suffe
2 CONC red a devastating defeat at Woyang 渦陽 (modern Mengcheng 蒙城, Anhui), Xie was removed from office. He returned home where he spent his leisure moments with another dismissed official Yu Zhongrong 庾仲容 (ca. 476–549). They often roamed
2 CONC  the countryside in an uncovered cart. Drinking heavily, clasping wooden clappers, they sang funeral songs. In 535, from Jingzhou, Xiao Yi 蕭繹 (508–555), Prince of Xiangdong, sent him a letter to console him. Xie Jiqing's reply to 
2 CONC the letter is preserved in his biography in the ''Liang shu''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Xie Jiqing's biography in the ''Nan shi'', he was recalled to official service again as director of the Court Watches in the household of the crown prince Xiao Gang 蕭綱 (503–551). He was soon promoted to assistant dire
2 CONC ctor of the left in the Department of State Affairs. He died of illness while serving as senior administrator to the left grand master of splendid happiness. However, his biography in the Liang shu says that after Xie was dismisse
2 CONC d from office and returned home, he did not receive any further official employment. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From an early age Xie Jiqing was a diligent scholar. He showed such remarable intelligence as a young boy he was labeled a ''shen tong'' 神童 or “child prodigy.” Xie Jiqing had a profound knowledge of ancient institutions and preced
2 CONC ents. While he was serving in the Department of State Affairs, the learned scholar Xu Mian 徐勉 (466–535), who was vice director of the Department of State Affairs, often consulted him on such matters. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Xie Jiqing's biography in the ''Liang shu'', his collected works circulated in the late Southern Dynasties. However, no collection is listed in the monograph on bibliography in the ''Sui shu'', and thus his writings m
2 CONC ust have been lost before the Tang. His only extant work is the letter he sent to Xiao Yi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 444. 
2 CONT * Ding Fulin 丁福林. Dong Jin Nanchao Xieshi wenxue jituan 東晉南朝謝氏文學集團, 252–55. Harbin: Heilongjiang jiaoyu chubanshe, 1998.
2 CONT
