1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Bo (Traditional Chinese: ???) (personal name unknown) (d. 147 BC) was an empress during the Han Dynasty. She was the first wife of Emperor Jing.
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Bo was from the same clan as Empress Dowager Bo, who married her to her grandson, then-Crown Prince Qi during the reign of her son, Emperor Wen. She carried the title of crown princess during the reign of her father-in-law
1 CONC . When her husband became emperor in 157 BC, she was created empress, but she was not favored by her husband, and she had no sons. It was as a consequence of this that Consort Li's son Liu Rong was made crown prince.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Grand Empress Dowager Bo died in 155 BC, Empress Bo lost her source of support within the palace. In 151 BC, her husband deposed her. She died four years later.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Empress Dou Empress of Western Han Dynasty
1 CONT 157 BC–151 BC Succeeded by
1 CONT Empress Wang Zhi
0 @N6000000002188144450@ NOTE Liu Sheng
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Liu Sheng (?? d. 113 BC) was a Chinese prince of the Han dynasty. He reigned from 157 BC to 141 BC. His father was Emperor Jing.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died before his wife and was interred in a mound 95 miles from Beijing, in Hebei. His tomb was unearthed during construction in 1968. The total space of the tomb is about 2,700 cubic metres, and vaguely resembles a human body. 
1 CONC He and his wife, Tou Wan, were both buried inside jade burial suits which consisted of 2,498 plates of solid jade connected with two and a half pounds of gold wires. 2,800 funerary offerings were also unearthed.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was an ancestor of Liu Bei, ruler of the Kingdom of Shu in the Three Kingdoms period.
0 @N6000000002188144137@ NOTE Empress Chen Jiao
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Chen Jiao (Traditional Chinese: ??; pinyin: Chén Jiao) was an empress during Han Dynasty. She was the first wife of Emperor Wu of Han, but was deposed in 130 BCE. Her father was Chen Wu (??), the Marquess of Tangyi. Her mo
1 CONC ther was Emperor Wu's aunt Princess Liu Piao (??), making her and her husband cousins[1]. They married when he was still the Prince of Jiaodong under his father Emperor Jing, and the marriage did much to enhance his political posi
1 CONC tion, to allow him to later become crown prince over his older brother Liu Rong. When he became emperor in 141 BC, she was created empress[2].
1 CONT
1 CONT Initially, Emperor Wu's love for her was so great that he bragged that he would build a golden house for her, inspiring the Chinese idiom "putting Jiao in a golden house" (????), which, however, later became a term for keeping a m
1 CONC istress rather than a wife. She eventually lost favor because she did not bear a son, despite spending over 90 million currency coins in seeking treatment for her infertility[3]. Jealous of Consort Wei Zifu (???), who gave birth t
1 CONC o a princess within one year, Empress Chen eventually retained witches to use witchcraft, in the attempt to restore her husband's love to her and curse other concubines (Consort Wei in particular). After this was discovered, she w
1 CONC as deposed according to imperial laws[4] and put under house arrest (although, at least according to Emperor Wu, supplied with all the daily living necessities that an empress would receive). The witches and other people in the wi
1 CONC tchcraft, some 300, were executed in the crackdown[5].
1 CONT
1 CONT Consort Wei was made empress several years later when she bore Emperor Wu his first son, after giving birth to three princesses. The former Empress Chen, now living in the cheerless, lonely Long Gate Palace (???), attempted to reg
1 CONC ain Emperor Wu's sympathy by hiring the famous poet Sima Xiangru (????) to compose a song, which is later known as The Ode of Long Gate (???)[6]. Although some later scholoars claimed that Emperor Wu was so touched by the poetic w
1 CONC ords that he revisited (ex-)Empress Chen and loved her again[7], these claims are likely to be just historically inaccurate bluffs -- all attempts by Chen Jiao to recapture Emperor Wu's heart were unsuccessful. She died about 20 y
1 CONC ears after she was deposed, but the exact year is not known. Her family, plagued by scandalous bad behaviours, also suffered downfall soon after she was deposed.[8]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
